<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wesel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wesel is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where the Lippe joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. A Hanseatic town in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, it became a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Calvinists in the 16th century and later a major Prussian fortress. It was almost totally destroyed in 1945 during the Allied crossing of the Rhine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wesel.jpg" alt="Wesel" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Peter Schaefer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wesel_willibrordi_dom_chor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wesel stands at the confluence of the Lippe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, and it is the capital of the Wesel district. The city grew from a Franconian manor first recorded in the 8th century. In the 12th century the Duke of Clèves took possession of it, and in the 15th century it became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. It was second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in the lower Rhine region as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>, a clearing station for the transshipment and trading of goods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Walloon community with French-language church services was noted in Wesel in 1545, and a French Huguenot commune was founded there in 1688. In 1590 the Spanish captured the city after a four-year siege, and it changed hands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and the Spanish several times during the Eighty Years War. A French force under Louis II de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, Prince de Condé, captured it in 1672. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> inherited Wesel in 1609 but could not take control of it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678.</p><p>In 1805 the city was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> under the Treaty of Schönbrunn. It became part of the Grand Duchy of Berg under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> in 1806 and was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> itself in January 1808. The French fortified it heavily, building the Citadelle Napoleon at Büderich, which was renamed Fort Blücher after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, when the city passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>As a strategic depot Wesel was bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and air raids on 16 to 19 February 1945 destroyed 97 per cent of the town. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> blew up the bridges over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Lippe, and on 23 March a bombardment by over 3,000 guns and a raid by RAF bombers prepared Operation Plunder, the Allied crossing of the Rhine. Operation Varsity dropped 18,000 troops behind the town. The population fell from almost 25,000 in 1939 to 1,900 in May 1945. In 1946 Wesel became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>. A restored 15th-century city hall, the Willibrordi-Dom and the citadel remain among its sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wesel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cleves was a wealthy trading land on the Rhine, with towns such as <b>Wesel</b>, Emmerich and Kalkar.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li></ul></section>
